{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/431634-a-theory-on-the-nature-of-the-judges-of-duat","id":431634,"title":"A Theory on the Nature of the Judges of Duat.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-03-07T12:31:09-08:00","id":431634,"title":"A Theory on the Nature of the Judges of Duat.","content":"So reading back on material in the Mummy:the Curse gameline after the Deceived extra material reignited a line of questioning that has been bothering me. C.A.S. has hinted that materials we already have give us some clues into the nature of what the Shan'iatu were trying to accomplish with both the Rite of Return and their setting up themselves as Judges of Death in Duat. Making these two posts that caught my eye on RPG.net during the release of Book of the Deceived. C.A.S.'s is in bold<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Originally Posted by <b>Reseru</b> The universe is a beautiful, precious place, and those gifts are despoiled by vengeful god-spirits<br /> <br /> <b>This is a glorious observation.<br /> <br /> As you were.<br /> <br /> C. </b> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Originally Posted by <b>Paradim</b> Answers and questions together make for great gaming.<br /> <br /> <b>I agree. Thankfully, Book of the Deceived provides both new questions and new answers. And it provides even more new answers for those willing to dig into the material and maybe read between the lines a little bit.</b><br /> <br /> C. </div> </div> </div>That kept on bothering me after poring over the original Mummy book at release, Guildhalls,etc. Of course a lot of us had initial thoughts at line release of, &quot;Ok 42 Shan'iatu, 42 Judges.&quot; Which of course Book of the Deceived took behind the woodshed. So, with what we have so far this is my theory on the Judges of Duat, in spoilers so folks that don't want material they haven't read yet spoiled for them of course.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <br /> Some things that kept cropping up, earlier in Mummy core and in book of the Deceived, kept sticking with me. Namely the implications that Ammut's nature and part to play with the Judges of Life wasn't honestly given to the Shan'iatu, that the Rite as Anpu says succeeds and would always succeed, and the nature of how Azar was brought to Duat. Alongside that we know each of the Guilds held a piece of what made the Rite work and the specific nature of how the Guild of the Restless Stars Shan'iatu were betrayed.<br /> <br /> I suspect that the fact they used the same thing they used to cut up Azar in the Rite of Return on themselves as well as the binding of the Shan'iatu to their servants are hints as to who the Judges actually are. Namely, they are the changed sorcerer protege guilds men and women of the Guild's themselves. The nature of the Decieved I think hints that what happened to them by the masters of the Shell was less a complete alteration of what was supposed to happen but a nasty little tweak on their part. The intent was to house the magical essences of the Shan'iatu, separated into certain servants souls down into Duat, where the Rite and the part played by the Restless Stars to then Name these mortal/temakh essences into the Judges, with the Restless Stars looking to be separate entities to lord over the others as per their plans.<br /> <br /> I think it is also possible that the Rite itself was, in some sense a deception itself on the part of the Judges of Life as well as Ammut. The Shan'iatu had betrayed, as best they could, their purpose to prepare people's souls to be worthy to enter into a paradise with the Judges of Life.<br /> <br /> I think it might be possible that the Rite, and the nature of the Rite is in some ways a play that the Shan'iatu could not really escape Fate and that the Judges of Duat it is possible in some way still perform this function. I am somewhat skeptical on this part as it makes the tone of the game overall quite different.</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2997,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/","user":"Guest","date":". 03-08-2015, 06:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-07T12:38:51-08:00","id":431654,"title":"","content":"I honestly just see it as &quot;the Shan'iatu wanted to imitate the Judges of Life and rule Duat alongside Azar as the Judges of Death, but their own hubris and Ammut's dishonesty lead to things going wrong.&quot; That may well be an oversimplification (or at the very least, an overly obvious reading), but it works well enough.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-07T13:47:18-08:00","id":431733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That may well be an oversimplification (or at the very least, an overly obvious reading), but it works well enough. </div> </div> </div>With the other gamelines I'd agree, but Mummy I think demands a bit more solidity. The history of Atlantis, Father Wolf or the God-Machine are generally stable elements of the setting. However Irem and the reality of the Judges have been deliberately built up as a monumental secret in the baseline setting. I don't think being vague about it will cut it really.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-07T14:02:24-08:00","id":431749,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/431634-a-theory-on-the-nature-of-the-judges-of-duat?p=431733#post431733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> With the other gamelines I'd agree, but Mummy I think demands a bit more solidity. The history of Atlantis, Father Wolf or the God-Machine are generally stable elements of the setting. However Irem and the reality of the Judges have been deliberately built up as a monumental secret in the baseline setting. I don't think being vague about it will cut it really.</div> </div> </div> </div>Particularly given that Atlantis and the Sundering are specifically distillations of a lot of different myths into something generally agreed-upon by their constituent supernaturals; what information we have on the Judges, by contrast, is specifically fragmentary.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}